  "easy": {
    "heading": "A Gentle Start to the Morning",
    "body": "We start off today with a nice little board that really lets us practice the basics. The first thing I noticed was the empty cell at 0,0 which is a great starting point because we know it has to be a zero. I paired it with 1,0 to form our first domino. From there, I looked at that little sum region at 1,2 and 1,3 which needed to add up to exactly 2. It felt like a quick win to get those settled early on.\n\nThe real key for this one was the equals region covering 1,0 and 2,0 and 3,0 and 3,1. Since we already knew 1,0 was part of our first move, it set a value that had to carry all the way down that line. It is like a little trail of breadcrumbs leading you across the board! I finished up by placing the 5,5 domino in the bottom corner because they both had to be equal at 3,3 and 4,3.\n\nOverall, it was a very satisfying way to wake up my brain. The less than constraint at 3,2 was the final piece of the puzzle, and once I saw that the value had to be under 2, everything just clicked into place. If you ever get stuck, just look for those equals regions first because they give away so much information.",
  },
  "medium": {
    "heading": "Finding My Flow in the Middle",
    "body": "Moving on to the medium puzzle, things got a bit more interesting with those empty spots scattered around. I immediately jumped to the sum of 0 at 0,2. It is basically a freebie because you know that cell has to be empty! I used the 1,2 and 0,2 domino to get that area started. It is always nice when the puzzle gives you a clear opening move like that.\n\nThe trickiest part for me was managing all the different regions that needed a sum of 3. We had one at 1,1 and another at 1,2 and 2,2. When you have multiple areas looking for the same total, you have to be really careful not to use up all your low-numbered dominoes too fast. I spent a minute looking at the 2,3 and 2,4 sum before realizing how they connected to the empty spots next to them.\n\nI ended up finishing the bottom half by focusing on the equals constraint at 3,4 and 4,4. By the time I got down there, I only had a few dominoes left, which actually made it easier to see what fit. Rodolfo Kurchan always makes these medium puzzles feel like a smooth dance where one move leads right into the next, and today was no exception.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "Breaking Down the Big One",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle today was a real workout for the old noggin! That massive equals region involving five different cells at 2,1 and 2,2 and 3,1 and 4,0 and 4,1 was quite intimidating at first glance. I had to sit there for a second and just look at my tray of dominoes to see which number appeared enough times to fill all those spots. It is definitely the heart of the whole board.\n\nAnother big challenge was the sum greater than 10 at 2,0 and 3,0. Since the highest a single cell can go is 6, I knew I needed some heavy hitters there. I eventually figured out that I needed to save my 5s and 6s for that specific spot. Once I realized that 4,0 and 3,0 were part of the same domino, it limited my choices even further and helped me clear out that left side of the grid.\n\nThe breakthrough happened when I looked at the sum of 10 at 0,4 and 0,5. There are only a couple of ways to make 10 with dominoes, so that really narrowed down my options for the top right corner. After that, the rest of the pips fell into place like a row of, well, dominoes! It was a tough one to wrap my head around, but man, it felt good to see that final solved screen.",
  },
  "tips": "Start by looking for the small target numbers like zero or one, as they really narrow down your options quickly. I always try to fill in the empty cells first since they act as an anchor for the rest of the board. Also, don't forget to keep a mental list of which dominoes you have already used so you do not try to place the same one twice!",
  "learned": "Today really showed me how helpful it is to look at the overlap between regions. In the hard puzzle, seeing that one big group of cells all had to be the same number was the only way I could narrow down which dominoes went where. It is like a big chain reaction once you get that first piece right. I also noticed that the greater than constraints are much easier to solve if you look at the highest available pips left in your tray.",
